  "ablation_size_label": "scored case: 100,000,000 floats (ranking_by LAST)",
  "takeaways": [
    "**Exact match, so radix:** the output must equal torch.sort; a radix sort on order-preserving key bits is exact by construction. One min/max pass lets every pass sort key - kmin: the scored data differ only in their low 24 bits, so 3 passes instead of 4, correct for any input.",
    "**The H100 #1 (129.7 us) is below the memory floor** (~240 us to read and write 800 MB): the leaders exploit the input's near-sortedness. The median is 2094 us; Crisp ranks 19/30 at 3749.5 us.",
    "**Onesweep (decoupled look-back) was correct but slower** here (1330 vs 1236 us per pass).",
    "**Two compiler bugs fixed on the way:** BUG 138 (ash/popcount were out-of-line calls on PTX) and BUG 145 (a while ending a let crashed the analyzer). Endeavour 200: tests/spec/200-sort-contest/sort-contest.md.",
    "**The downsweep is instruction-bound, not memory-bound:** -9% SASS gave -16% time (rev d). A run-time ash is a branch-free select chain; endeavour 211 added shift-left / shift-right / rotate-left / rotate-right (count mod width), and the per-key rotate became one funnel shift: -9.2% on the whole sort (rev j).",
    "**The histogram layout was the upsweep's limit:** digit-major per-tile counts hit 256 sectors per tile; tile-major made the upsweep 193 -> 150 us per pass (a fenced no-write probe: 136).",
    "**GPU MODE H100 runners differ by ~30%:** the same kernels ran 3150 vs 4063 us on two nodes, so a single leaderboard score cannot rank two kernels. Every A/B here runs all arms inside ONE test submission under eval.py's procedure (diagab_*.py). On one H100: v1c 4922, v1i 4063, v1j 3692 us; torch.sort (CUB) 5280.",
    "**Warp ballots replaced the two 4-bit splits (rev k):** a CUDA probe measured it before Crisp had a ballot (diagp_ballot.py), endeavour 212 added warp-ballot / warp-any? / warp-all?, and the Crisp downsweep went -14% on the whole sort (one H100: v1j 3693, v1k 3165 us).",
    "**Two traps on the way, both measured:** (1) shared memory is occupancy -- 8 KB more cost 830 us on H100 (3 vs 4 workgroups per SM), hence per-warp counters sized by (warp-size); (2) a global load inside a 16-round loop with a warp sync per round is 16 serial memory latencies -- loading the tile first took the ballot version from +7% to -14% against v1j."
  ],
  "beauty_entry": {
    "crisp_file": "07-sort-v2/sort_bitonic_beauty.crisp",
